Subject: Space and Crypto Opportunities (separate subjects)

Phil Fraering writes:

> It seems to have left out what I've been told is a currently viable
> concern (or was when I asked Nick about it): cryptographic
> authentification/"time-stamping" services. I've put some thought into
> this sort of thing as well, along different lines than the service
> that's apparently already in existance, and perhaps even acting in 
> some sort of cooperation with them (i.e. using them for higher-order
> verification).  Hmm... how much do y'all think it's prudent for me
> to say here? It's a long-term idea, but it's probably absurdly low-
> capital...

Haber and Stornetta's service has been discussed several times in this
group and on Cypherpunks. Bellcore holds a patent, so be careful.
Bellcore also plans a spin-off, to be located in the Bay Area, and
Scott Stornetta is moving out to be centrally involved.

(Aside on local, telecommuting, and Newfoundland issues: Telecommuting
is great for some types of jobs: extremely solitary jobs like writing,
art, etc., and extremely easily managed jobs, like remote processing
of insurance claims and the like. For things like high-tech startups,
it's usually a killer, at least for core players. The occasional
genius is "allowed" to live in Vail or Mazatlan (as Robert Widlar
did), but this is disruptive, counterproductive, and rare. Long days
spent in close contact with other team members is more the norm. Even
where I live, 40 miles from the Valley, is too far if I were in the
intensive phase of doing a start-up with associates in the Valley. I
respect Bill Garland's protestations that St. Johns (is that a city in
Newfoundland or a university?) is on the cutting edge (:-}), but I
predict serious problems that competitors in academic and high-tech
Meccas will not have.)

I see time-stamping as a possible product area....my enumeration of
several possible areas was by no means exhaustive. But getting into
the time-stamping business is not trivial, given the several year head
start of experts like Stornetta and Haber, the patent situation,
etc. Unless of course one is doing a kind of low-tech function of
having a storefront that takes documents, scans them, hashes them,
sends the hash to Bellcore for further hashing, etc. Given the curren
unawareness of such things, and no indications of explosive growth, I
would not think this to be too profitable. But maybe Phil can find a
real niche, something exotic like time-stamping drilling claims, or
something like that.

I wish him luck, and will now add him to my growing list of folks
interested in doing something commercial related to crypto!

> Anyway, if anyone has more information about this sort of thing,
> could they please post to the list?

Did this help? I know both Haber and Stornetta and can point you to
their papers.

> \But don't ask me what the obvious short-term investments are, or where
> /you should concentrate your efforts. I'll gladly shoot the breeze on
> \this topic for hours and hours, but I have no simple answers.
> 
> Good! If it were that simple, Bill Gates would have already made all
> the money on it by now! ;-) But it isn't, and there's some left for
> us peons ;-)

Making money takes real effort...I'm sure this is no surprise to
anyone--I'm only repeating it for emphasis, and to tie it in to
"logical depth" and AIT. To wit, the really _simple_ ways to make
money, the things advertised in the back of "Popular Nanotechnology,"
are almost never workable. To a few people, they are. 

One has to push out into knowledge space, out into relatively
unexplored regions. Then one can do things and make a fair amount of
money. Distance out into knowledge space is akin to logical depth, in
the Bennett sense...huge computational effort, learning, evolution to
get there (generally).

For example, this is what Bellcore will be able to do by capitalizing
on the work of Haber and Stornetta. This is just one of many such
examples, and is why I still think the best start ups are done by
using the money and resources of former employers, universities, etc.
This is how one can push out into new regions, beyond the "Fermi
level" of current knowledge.

(If this stuff looks cryptic to you, I'm sorry. It's the "knowledge
space" model I usually use in thinking about these things. It's
worked will for me for the last 10 years, and Gordon Moore of Intel
was even fond of it (I used the LSI product space of circuits,
lithography, and architecture to make many of my points). I wrote a
couple of pieces on this for the List a bit over a year ago, and Nick
Szabo also wrote some nice stuff on it too.)

> I'm waiting for Klaus!'s Crypto Zoology signature one of these days...

I'll work on it, but first I have to take care of some grendels coming
up the driveway.

--Kraut! mit Bratwurst


