Subject: Pigs at the Trough (was: "Soaking the Taxpayer")

Well, we could go round and round on this business of taking money
from the government.

I've said my piece, and am in general agreement with Stuart Card,
Blanc Weber, etc. Others, including Sandy Sandfort and Mark Desilets,
have a different view.

For relatively minor things like ARPA grants and SBIR money, which
gets doled out to _someone_, I can partly see the point of the folks
who say take the money and run (modulo the point that it's often a bad
deal to take the money anyway).

My main concern is when the money pot _does not in fact already exist_
and where asking for money, lobbying for it, promoting nanotech and
space crusades, Sixth Generation Computer Projects, and National Data
Highways becomes an _active_ step toward more theft, more slavery.

In other words, there's a big difference (well, maybe _not_ so big,
once you think about it some more) between "Here's a bunch of money
we're gonna give out to whoever asks for it first" and "Convince us
that your program is a good thing and we'll go out and steal some
money for it."

Likewise, I don't really argue that NASA subcontractors should be shot
or blown up (as someone once claimed I did), just that NASA ought to
be killed off posthaste and that I wouldn't weep if a few disasters
sped the process along.

All abstractions aside, and with all due respect to my good friends
Sandy, Nick, and Mark, where the issue really comes up for me is what
I have been hinting about in several of my posts: funding of
nanotechnology.

We've discussed this before on this list, and I've had numerous
discussions with Eric Drexler, Mark Miller, Marc Stiegler, Keith
Henson, and many others about this issue. While Drexler is too much of
a free market advocate to clearly say "The free market has failed--I
want a government program to fund me," this is the undertone of many
of his comments. I won't speculate about his motives, which I'm sure
are honorable. I think he'w wrong, for both practical and "ethical"
reasons.

My late friend Phil Salin, who often joined me on my side in these
debates with Eric, had a wonderful concept he called the "slave-life
method of accounting." Briefly, since I've written about this before
here, one estimates the average American will earn about one million
dollars in his working lifetime ($25K per year for 40 years, in 1994
dollars). This is working 40 hours a week. A full-time slave might be
worked twice as hard, but wouldn't be as efficient (exhaustion, anger,
etc.), so I'll stick with the million dollar figure. You can use $1.5
million if you're feeling sadistic.

Thus, the "noble goal" of "putting a man on the moon" cost us around
75,000 slave-lives. That is, the entire working output of 75,000 men
and women went into this, as the Apollo program cost about $75 billion
(in 1994 dollars).

Taxes were collected at the point of a gun, ultimately, so that these
slave-lives could be spent for the enjoyment of Lyndon Johnson, some
astronauts, a bunch of contractors, and to rub the noses of the
Soviets in the dirt.

(Yes, many Americans enjoyed it. In which case they could have and
should have paid for it voluntarily.)

Now I'm not saying that Mark Desilets is holding a gun to my head in
collecting his ARPA or SBIR money, or whatever. All I'm saying is that
it's a slippery slope when one says the public trough is OK for one's
own projects or pet interests.

This is one more reason I'm generally against "Big Science" and have
called for the death of the Supercollider (score one), the death of
the Space Station (a pretty good chance, as it's shrinking to the
point of meaninglessness), and the deaths of the Data Highway, the
Human Genome Project, and several other efforts.

And, yes, I did indeed razz my friends for applying to ARPA for the
research grant they got. Their first words when they called to tell me
they'd gotten it were "Tim, I know you're gonna like this, but..." But
having razzed them about it, and having seen them also argue with Eric
Drexler against his new-found fascination with Al Gore and the Joint
Chiefs of Staff, I am not ready to throw them onto the dustbin of
history.

So, take the money if you feel you must. Yes, if you don't take it,
others will. But then the same argument applies to any and all
robbers who offer to distribute the booty, right?

--Tim May


