Date: Thu, 6 Jan 1994 00:00:35 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: trans- and post-

Anton Sherwood: 
> I take "posthuman" to mean whatever we will be after the
> Singularity, when all bets are off; "transhuman" suggests
> incremental improvements on our natural condition, 

This doesn't quite work for me since I don't recognize any
single "Singularity", but rather a multiplicity of
singularities ranging from next week's weather to whether
uploads will come before strong AI's or vice versa.  If
uploads win, even the post-uploading world is "pre-Singularity"
in the sense that it can be partially predicted: cf.  Robin Hanson's 
economic analyses uploading, my Uploading & Sexual Engineering 
essay, etc.  (Robin's analysis, but not mine, also applies
to AI's, but I'll get to that some other time). 

For convenience I categorize "transhuman" as the stage between
cryonics reanimation and uploading, "posthuman" after uploading.
Transhumanism might include various neural and implanted
computer brain expansions, mechanical cyborg forms,
and the end of nearly all fatal conditions
including aging.  Smart drugs is a form of transhumanism
many of us might see in full flower before we're deanimated.

Some folks think uploading will arrive before deanimation,
or at least before reanimation, but I demur.  Realigning
cracked frozen synapses and preventing reperfusion ischemia
strikes me as much less complex than full quantum-mechanical 
simulation of synapses, hormones, and sensory organs; especially 
for those of us fortunate enough to make it to early 21st-century 
century freezing techniques instead of today's.  Early and badly 
deanimated cryonicists may go straight to the posthuman, uploaded
stage.

