Subject: Accepting Gifts vs. Giving Gifts

Some good points on both sides of the philosophical debate, whether
it is immoral to accept gifts from a thief.  I do agree that trading 
a thief's gifts for support of the operations of that thief
is immoral.   I think accepting such a gift with no mutual obligation
to the thief is fine, although I acknowledge the practical difficulty of 
obtaining or sustaining such a one-way relationship in many cases.
One might argue that the recipient should attempt to find and
recompense the thief's victims, but this point is moot if it is
the victim receiving the gift.

I also disagree with the claim that gift recipients are primarily 
responsible for the existence of the thief.  I say the bulk of that 
burden lies on those who fund the thief in the first place: those
of us who give in to robbery and extortion, quietly handing over major
fractions of our income, without attempting to fight off the
thief or attempting to bring the thief to justice.  The 
fundamental immorality lies there, at the source of the
thief's livelihood.  And as most of us are guilty of accepting the
gifts of thieves, so most of us are also guilty of funding the thieves
without a fight.

